# Analysis plan (written before the main runs; revisions are appended at the end, never silently)

Source: `idea-repo` Brief rev. 4 (predictions P1–P5, acceptance rule), `story-repo` Outline rev. 1 (claims C0–C7),
objective `experiment-repo`#1. Everything below is code in this repository (`src/gate`, `scripts/`).

## 1. Harness
* **Estimand**: GATE τ = mean_i Y_i(1) − mean_i Y_i(0), evaluated by running each outcome function at z=1 and z=0
  (`mech.exact_tau`; equals 1 up to 1.3e-4 for mechanism (e), whose K=30-step truncation is part of the DGP).
  Errors are (τ̂ − τ)/τ with τ = 1.
* **Graphs**: SBM (blocks of ≈100 nodes, 30 % of edge mass between blocks), ER, Barabási–Albert (m = d/2),
  Watts–Strogatz (β = 0.1), n = 3000 unless stated; socfb-Stanford3 and SNAP ego-Facebook (sha256 verified against
  `data.md`), giant component only. On real graphs degree is varied by independent edge thinning (target mean degree
  5/20/80; ego-Facebook has 43.7, so "80" means the full graph there); clustering coefficient, maximum degree and n of every
  realised graph are stored (`gmeta`) and reported with every degree effect.
* **Mechanisms** (all calibrated to τ = 1; spillover share s ∈ {0.25, 0.5, 0.75}, plus the s = 0 null, one column):
  (a) (1−s)z + s f; (b) (1−s)z + s c/d̄ (count, degree scaled); (c) (1−s)z + s g(f), g saturating with κ = 3;
  (d) (1−s)z + s·1[f ≥ 0.6]; (e) (1−ρ)Σ_{k≤30} ρ^k W^k z with ρ = s; (f) (1−s)z + (s/2)f + (s/2)zf.
  f = treated-neighbour fraction, c = count. Outcome = a_i + this part (+ noise).
* **Baselines a_i** (SD 1): iid (default), degree-correlated (0.8 standardised log-degree + 0.6 iid), community-correlated
  (0.8 block effect + 0.6 iid), none (sanity only).
* **Noise**: ε iid N(0,1) scaled by σ = m·SD(noise-free outcome of that column), m ∈ {0.25, 0.5, 1} (SD from 30 pilot
  assignments per graph draw; tabulated per mechanism × s in `calibration.csv`).
* **Replicate structure**: graph draws (G = 10 synthetic; 1 for fixed real graphs) × assignment draws (R = 300; 3000
  on real graphs) × one noise draw per assignment (common to all columns/estimators). Every Tier-G estimator is a linear
  functional τ̂ = w'y given the assignment, so the signal part w'y₀ and the noise part w'ε are stored separately and recombined
  at any m; the exact conditional noise variance (mσ_j)²·‖w‖² is stored too (‖w‖² = L/n for the regression estimators).
  This gives the variance split Var(τ̂) = E[σ²‖w‖²] (noise) + Var(w'y₀) (assignment/graph) exactly.
* **Designs**: Bernoulli(p), p ∈ {0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9}; graph-cluster randomisation: cluster-level Bernoulli(p=½),
  clusters from Louvain on the *observed* graph at resolution γ ∈ {0.3, 1, 3} (one rule on every graph: the same three
  resolutions, reported, not tuned per graph) or planted SBM blocks (oracle).
* **Edge noise**: outcomes on the true graph, all features/designs/exposure probabilities on a graph with q ∈ {0.1, 0.3}
  of true edges removed (each node keeps ≥ 1 edge).

## 2. Estimators run in this stage (Tier G, O)
Tier G (generic information, graph known, no mechanism parameter): `dm`; `hajek75` and `hajek100` (Hájek exposure-mapping
estimators with exposure "z=1 and ≥ q of neighbours treated" vs "z=0 and ≥ q control", q = 0.75 / 1; exposure
probabilities exact for Bernoulli, 1000-draw Monte Carlo for cluster designs; undefined (NaN) when an exposure group is
empty — the defined-rate is reported); `chin` (own z + f, the reporting specification); ladder `quad` (+f²), `spline` (linear
spline in f, knots at observed quartiles of f), `count` (+c/d̄), `hop2` (+2-hop fraction f₂), `inter` (+z·f).
Tier O: `or_known` = correct functional form with the true parameter (κ, θ, ρ=s known; (a) = `chin`, (f) = `inter`), and
`or_fit` = the same form with the single shape parameter profiled by least squares on a grid (κ∈{.5..8}, θ∈{.1..9}, ρ∈{0..0.85}),
evaluated at m = 0.5 only (it is not linear in y). Uncertainty of `chin`/`dm`: HC0 sandwich SE and
unit bootstrap (B = 100; cluster bootstrap under cluster designs) at m = 0.5 on the first 30 assignments of each graph draw (300 on real graphs).
Not in this stage (separate runs, subset of cells, fewer draws, listed in §7): Tier A.

## 3. Predictions the data are held to, derived before running
Regular-degree, tree-like, Bernoulli(p), linear OLS on (z, f) — z ⟂ f, so τ̂_pop = Cov(y,z)/Var z + Cov(y,f)/Var f.
* P1 (variance): Var(τ̂ | z) = σ²_resid L/n with L = n‖w‖² ≈ (1+d)/(p(1−p)); the noise part is exact by construction, so the test is
  (i) realised L vs the formula, (ii) the assignment part Var(w'y₀) (fixed a_i and model misfit), (iii) the departure
  realised Var/(σ²L/n) by graph family/design. Fit log RMSE on log n, log d, log p(1−p) where noise dominates.
* P2 (bias, population value of the linear fit, spillover bias = β_f − (g(1)−g(0))):
  (a) 0. (b) s(HM(d)/AM(d) − 1), HM/AM the harmonic/arithmetic mean degree ratio (degree-heterogeneity only).
  (c),(d) s·[Cov(h(f),f)/Var(f) − 1] with f_i ~ Bin(d_i,p)/d_i, computed exactly per node from the realised degree sequence
  (`analysis/predict_bias.py`): ≈ −½h''(1−2p) − (1/6)h'''[(1−p)³+p³] for smooth h, independent of d to leading order and with
  O(1/d) corrections; for the threshold h = 1[f ≥ θ] the slope is s·(density of f at θ)·… — it **does depend on d**: for θ=0.6 > p
  the fit sees a steep slope when the support (width ∝ d^{-½}) straddles θ (overshoot, positive bias), and tends to −s (no
  slope, whole jump missed) as d→∞. This corrects the Outline's "bias independent of d" for thresholds; it holds for smooth responses.
  (e) ≈ −ρ² (the mass of terms k ≥ 2 of the diffusion that (z,f) cannot see), smaller with clustering (common neighbours). (f) −s(1−2p)/2.
  Flexible/other estimators: trees extrapolate flat (Tier A), polynomials (quad/spline) do not remove the bias for (d).
* P3 (p): variance ∝ 1/(p(1−p)); bias minimiser: p=½ for (a),(b),(e) is flat in p, for (f) at ½, for (c) shifted below ½ if
  g''<0, g'''>0 (κ=3) — compare the exact prediction in P2.
* P4 (design): see §4, C5.

